    {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186064250,
        "id": "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7",
        "title": "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "15d0a57a-da2d-543e-928d-7290d4d1d2ac",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3}{|3 x|\\sqrt{9 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWork on a connected interval where $|3 x|>1$.\n\nChoose the antiderivative. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=3 x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(3)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWork on a connected real interval where $|3 x|>1$.\n\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. The outside factor is $3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=1$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{3}{|3 x|\\sqrt{9 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1} \\left|{u}\\right|}\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1} \\left|{u}\\right|}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1} \\left|{u}\\right|}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(3 x \\right)}+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "9a2961c8-2e97-538a-939d-ebb005be6318",
